summary:
- topic: Student and Staff Recognitions
  text: The board celebrated numerous students for their achievements in academics
    (Top Scholars), music (Allstate honors), and robotics (international competition
    success). Additionally, staff members were recognized for their outstanding service,
    including retirees and Heather Altenburg, the Maine Principals’ Association Administrative
    Assistant of the Year.
- topic: Superintendent's Report on Enrollment and Facilities
  text: Superintendent Angela Atkinson Duina reported a significant decline in student
    enrollment, which is projected to impact future state subsidies. She also outlined
    critical facility needs, including security upgrades, deferred maintenance for
    HVAC and roofing at elementary schools, and necessary improvements to the stadium
    complex.
- topic: FY25 Budget Approval
  text: The board approved the Fiscal Year 2025 budget, totaling $69,279,601. This
    included detailed allocations for various educational categories, local contributions,
    and non-state funded debt service, following a comprehensive review process.
- topic: Policy Rescissions
  text: Several outdated or redundant personnel policies were officially rescinded,
    a housekeeping measure aimed at streamlining the policy manual and ensuring clarity
    on current district guidelines.
- topic: Elementary School Boundaries and Configuration Analysis
  text: The board approved a recommendation to conduct an analysis of attendance zones
    and viable elementary grade level configurations. This analysis will be guided
    by a consultant, selected by a committee including board members and residents,
    with findings presented by January 2025.
- topic: Equity in Resource Allocation
  text: A motion was passed to ensure that schools with disproportionate percentages
    of economically disadvantaged students receive additional resources, including
    staffing, to support student learning. A full report on this allocation will be
    provided to the board by August 2024.
- topic: Controlled Choice Model and Policy Review
  text: The board decided to secure a consultant to develop a proposal for a controlled
    choice model, exploring highly successful magnet programs that foster innovation
    and equity. Additionally, a new policy will be developed requiring the board to
    review and approve attendance zones every three years, with annual reports on
    demographic impacts.
